The first typed checkpoint learned to execute public functions, but lost several earlier catalog tasks when compared with the previous specialist. We continued its shared rank-32 adapter on 2,527 turns. The additions included 320 pairs from eight separate training settings. Each pair reversed which item had the larger quantity. A margin loss at the first differing answer token supplemented normal next-token training. The optimization used 1,000 eight-example updates and 100,669 supervised token positions. Frozen-prefix preparation took 185.13 s and optimization took 217.38 s.

#figure(image("joint-tools.svg",width: 97%),caption: [A single two-pass checkpoint answered all four panels. The earlier checkpoint has measurements on the first three. Each success requires the complete tool path and the correct answer, or the appropriate direct response or clarification.])

The candidate completed 112/132 tasks correctly against 15/132 for untouched Base, with 98 paired gains and one loss. Total time was 69.55 versus 323.35 s and peak MLX allocation was 1.09 versus 1.66 GiB. The candidate emitted 4,863 tokens and Base emitted 21,830. Source scores were 26/30 versus 5/30 on the original catalog, 42/50 versus 7/50 on the second, 18/22 versus 0/22 on publisher numerical executions and 26/30 versus 3/30 across four new schema domains. All registered family floors passed. The fresh domains use the project's controlled world generator. The publisher questions provide a separate human-authored endpoint.

On the 102 tasks previously measured with the earlier typed checkpoint, the new model solved 86 against 70. It gained 21 previously unsolved tasks and lost five previously correct ones. All five losses involved comparing quantities. Net gains were four on the first catalog, eleven on the second and one on public numerical execution. The paired evidence distinguishes a better aggregate system from preservation of each individual behavior.

A separate structural test compared the new and earlier recurrent adapters on 72 publisher questions at a common 384-token budget. The earlier model reproduced all 48 previously measured output streams. On the original single-function, original competing-schema and new competing-schema panels the new model scored 15, 16 and 17 out of 24. The prior model scored 16, 18 and 18. The four changes were all losses ($p=0.125$). Two newly wrong calls failed schema validation and two were well typed with incorrect argument choices. The new model completed all 72 generations in 27.90 s; the prior model completed 71 in 31.03 s. The prespecified retention floors closed replacement of the earlier adapter across all turns.

The matched one-pass continuation received the same 2,527 turns, 100,669 target positions, 802,816 trainable parameters and 1,000 updates. It completed 108/132 sessions in 60.21 s. Two passes exactly replayed the earlier 112/132 output trajectories in 69.33 s. Eight one-pass failures became correct, and four one-pass successes became wrong (exact paired $p=0.388$). The original, grounded, public and fresh panel scores for one pass were 24, 41, 18 and 25. The extra pass took 15.14% more time against a registered ceiling of 15%. Optimization took 136.78 s for one pass and 217.38 s for two. This experiment establishes a large difference from untouched Base and leaves the contribution of the second pass uncertain.

